name  = json
shape = parse a document with the ecosystem's parser, then walk the whole tree
args  = $DATA/records.json
claim = a different question from every other row here. This is `contrib/json`,
        a parser written in Mere, against parsers that are native code shipped
        with their runtime -- Go's encoding/json, V8's JSON.parse, Ruby's JSON
        and Python's json are none of them written in the language whose row
        they occupy. So this measures "a language plus what its ecosystem hands
        you", which is a real question when choosing one, and not the same
        question as "how fast is code this language compiles".
        No C or Rust row: C has no standard JSON parser, and Rust's is a crate
        this suite cannot fetch. Hand-rolling either would compare parsers
        rather than ecosystems, which is the one thing this row is not for.
        READ THIS ROW TWICE. On wall clock Mere is fastest, and that is a real
        answer to "how long until I have the number". But subtract each row's
        process startup, which benchmarks/startup isolates (mere 5 ms, Go 6 ms,
        Python 35 ms, Ruby 176 ms, Node 200 ms), and the ordering changes:
        contrib/json does its ~124 ms of work against V8's ~87 ms. Mere's
        parser is SLOWER than the fastest native one here and wins the row
        anyway because the runtime carrying that parser costs more to start
        than the parsing costs to do. Both halves are true and they answer
        different questions.
        The document is sized so the parse dominates. At an eighth of it the
        parse was 16 ms of Mere and every dynamic row was mostly startup -- a
        benchmark whose signal is buried under a cost another row already
        measures is not measuring what its claim says.
        The checksum walks the WHOLE tree, so a parser cannot win by being lazy
        about the parts nobody reads. Numbers in the input are integers only
        and nesting is one level deep: `contrib/json` parses `JNum of int`, and
        a document with 1.5e3 in it would be timing one implementation's error
        path against five implementations' happy path.

# 139.8 MB at v0.1.323, for an 11.8 MB document -- a ~12x expansion into the
# parse tree, which is what a `json list` of `(str * json)` pairs costs when
# every node is a region allocation and nothing is freed. Go holds 90 MB for
# the same tree with a collector underneath it.
#
# It was 169.2 MB at v0.1.321 and 159.0 at v0.1.322. Attribution by return
# address named where the rest went, and two of the three biggest sites were
# fixable: 50.3%-of-binarytrees'-kind of empty nodes (v0.1.322, shared nullary
# constructors, -10.2 MB here) and 1.12 million calls to `str_unescape` that
# copied a string with no escape in it into an identical one (v0.1.323,
# -19.2 MB, which matched the attribution to the byte). The largest remaining
# site is contrib/json's own `rev_aux`, which allocates a fresh spine to
# reverse its accumulator -- 28%, and a library question rather than a language
# one.
#
# The floor sits well below the current value on purpose. Its job is to catch a
# meter that stopped seeing its subject, not to argue with the next
# improvement -- and it has already caught one legitimate halving by being too
# close (binarytrees, v0.1.322).
alloc_min = 67108864
alloc_max = 167772160
